WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.480
A tulip poplar seed weighs, on average, less than 0.1 g.

2
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.780
One tulip poplar in a Virginia forest grew so big that scientists estimated about

3
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.920
10,000 kg of dry wood.

4
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.140
That is an estimate from a model, not a weighing.

5
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.500
So where did all that wood come from?

6
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:24.213
A deer cannot make its own food.

7
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:28.373
It has to eat. A plant does not eat, yet it grows.

8
00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:32.053
A producer is a living thing that makes its own food.

9
00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:35.673
Green plants, algae and phytoplankton are producers.

10
00:00:36.973 --> 00:00:39.713
Phytoplankton are tiny producers that drift in water.

11
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:43.067
Producers make food by photosynthesis.

12
00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:49.547
Photosynthesis uses the energy of sunlight to turn carbon dioxide and water into

13
00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:53.967
sugar and oxygen. The energy in sunlight is radiant energy.

14
00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:56.647
It is energy that travels as light.

15
00:00:57.507 --> 00:01:02.407
In this summary the sugar is glucose, a small sugar molecule that stores energy.

16
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.590
In plants and algae, photosynthesis happens inside a chloroplast.

17
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.630
A chloroplast is a green part of a cell, mostly in leaves.

18
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:16.290
It holds chlorophyll, a green pigment that captures radiant energy.

19
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.730
Roots take in water from the soil.

20
00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.130
Leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air, through tiny openings.

21
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:29.010
Carbon dioxide is only a tiny part of air, about 0.04 percent.

22
00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:33.123
Once a plant has sugar, it can do three things with it.

23
00:01:33.643 --> 00:01:34.683
It can use it now.

24
00:01:35.423 --> 00:01:36.563
It can store it as starch.

25
00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:42.043
Or it can rearrange it into other compounds, such as the wood of its trunk and the

26
00:01:42.043 --> 00:01:43.043
parts of new leaves.

27
00:01:43.723 --> 00:01:48.263
Light, carbon dioxide and temperature change how fast photosynthesis goes.

28
00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:51.817
So where did the tulip poplar's wood come from?

29
00:01:52.477 --> 00:01:56.817
The tree built it mostly from carbon dioxide from the air, and from water.

30
00:01:58.057 --> 00:02:02.337
Leaves used radiant energy to join them into sugar, and the tree rebuilt the sugar

31
00:02:02.337 --> 00:02:05.737
into wood. The soil gave water and a few minerals.

32
00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:09.537
So the soil did not need to shrink by thousands of kilograms.
